Great question! If two interfaces have the same method signature (e.g., display()), and a class implements both interfaces,
there’s no conflict as long as the class provides its own implementation of the method.

✅ Example:
java
Copy
Edit
interface A {
    void display();
}

interface B {
    void display();
}

class MyClass implements A, B {
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Display from MyClass");
    }
}
👇 Usage:
java
Copy
Edit
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyClass obj = new MyClass();
        obj.display();  // Output: Display from MyClass
    }
}
✅ Only one method is implemented in the class, and it satisfies both interfaces.

🧠 Why No Conflict?
Interfaces only declare methods (no body).

So, as long as the class implements display() once, it's valid for both.